#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119637 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/id #uuid "005f8f3f-89eb-5e99-bf0f-82b67329661a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4910" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y+3}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y-(-3)}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,-3,2)$ and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 5,3,1 \\rangle.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119638 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/id #uuid "897aa930-02ca-5f79-95d0-5ee11420da97" :question/math-academy-id "e-4911" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t \\\\ y=4t \\\\ z=-2+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ -2+3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,0,-2)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle -2, 4, 3 \\rangle.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z-(-2)}{3} = t, \t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z+2}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119639 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/id #uuid "df314d1d-56c8-5503-b632-8a425b47070f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4912" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure below.\n\n![](images/79/792bf09ff2546903b863a879a2518c42bbd30fdfce652bd4e22b1ad19aefc7c9.png)" :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points ![](images/aa/aa4328c3ce96a1b9871e71f5cc27e255e47d186fe5848ea0f7d43b324d41a2d6.svg) and ![](images/9a/9a6a4549ad0127d861d9a5c0a1ceff0b41b7c06b648a327779f31b325ea5800e.svg) Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector ![](images/e6/e6db167bc32b19da05d87b24e9b910b80d32a1ef9e5965ab4cb5217a1fac4730.svg) which is given by\n![](images/e1/e17614c58be91b6165e40be1c489685e14898756db2d6a41bf80b638e666fc51.svg)\n\nUsing the point ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) and the vector ![](images/e6/e6db167bc32b19da05d87b24e9b910b80d32a1ef9e5965ab4cb5217a1fac4730.svg), we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n![](images/8d/8df9f1471a16c7b99e55290a41fe97641d62cef3bf5ba0e57e7fe6c029e98807.svg)\nor equivalently\n![](images/9e/9e688c295265c270ba7e1f66fc13ceebe9f6385ea45000c6f42c204d3fb6ba50.svg)\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because ![](images/74/7435bec0e47e7cfafdcbdbf0763b77d55a16344504663512cbbcf8b75a50381f.svg) involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) component always maintains the constant value ![](images/ef/efcfb7a56c85727ded8a9d5c9bd75fd79ac0bc20ba202c2da6aaca3c4e3ba503.svg) For example, our two points ![](images/aa/aa4328c3ce96a1b9871e71f5cc27e255e47d186fe5848ea0f7d43b324d41a2d6.svg) and ![](images/de/dee63698dc962e270612a470eb5e1eb23ffcbd72c8f9d787bc7eb927ff05f1c6.svg) both have ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)-coordinates of ![](images/ef/efcfb7a56c85727ded8a9d5c9bd75fd79ac0bc20ba202c2da6aaca3c4e3ba503.svg)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the ![](images/74/7435bec0e47e7cfafdcbdbf0763b77d55a16344504663512cbbcf8b75a50381f.svg) part and instead write ![](images/6b/6be447d259b9b63c2cdd7adc4107d7c96ed4df1730890fc1bb5ce7cd585b8ea6.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92f849bc9c2fc37e54e4ecf4901b06624536d835db97d31f94ab77573d6e401b.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119656 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "74fb2af1-d791-5687-98cc-0b2db5bd8464" :question/math-academy-id "e-5021" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n![](images/5c/5cb83f5140e0f687997fea631f78bb9d5cf7d6cbeef52fe746f4abcba9230a1a.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/19/1998dafc905e62617ac4c8f57285a0d75cf0ef8bddf1e59be7eeea9149485017.svg)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "299601c1-c189-56c9-a263-8a4e23810703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413210} {:answer/id #uuid "38f146eb-7159-527b-9b0e-80360837fbdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413213} {:answer/id #uuid "4aeef7c0-439a-5ccb-8d85-ceb73b3ed2da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413216} {:answer/id #uuid "7acfe4e3-373d-5d11-a879-57c9a43b56c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413226} {:answer/id #uuid "8393d971-684a-53d4-b181-6839d28c8304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "299601c1-c189-56c9-a263-8a4e23810703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413210} :answer-field/id #uuid "a00f7c00-cf28-532d-ba2d-23cd87582c37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1146c0df-94bc-5e1e-b7ad-b9815bc6197e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127071" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x-7}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z+3}{-7}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{-7},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(7,2,-3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨3,4,-7⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "089f0314-d541-57da-8474-803ac5e9d9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=13+7t \\\\ y=-3+2t \\\\ z=1-5t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413206} {:answer/id #uuid "4c141cec-318c-5fa6-984e-d5d96ba7c25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413217} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e9eb55-76d8-5834-b077-9d8e4ba666d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=13-7t \\\\ y=-2+3t \\\\ z=1+5t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413235} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d95702-203e-5dd9-8f89-b3e9ad2f7948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=11+7t \\\\ y=2-3t \\\\ z=1+t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413236} {:answer/id #uuid "d117c2e1-a8ab-51ed-866a-b2f0126b6bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=13+7t \\\\ y=2+3t \\\\ z=1-5t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5e9eb55-76d8-5834-b077-9d8e4ba666d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=13-7t \\\\ y=-2+3t \\\\ z=1+5t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413235} :answer-field/id #uuid "10bab257-9e49-5588-bdbd-e51321b62eff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2f7b0c6-34b9-5cdd-a2c3-dccbfbacb488" :question/math-academy-id "q-127073" :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x-13}{-7}=\\frac{y+2}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-13}{-7}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(13,-2,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-7,3,5⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=13-7t \\\\ y=-2+3t \\\\ z=1+5t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c21e0bb-ee8c-5fa2-ad4c-8c70768e7b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=7t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=-6-8t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413218} {:answer/id #uuid "898d0828-6322-5cc9-9782-a0f2a96d4581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=7t \\\\ y=3+2t \\\\ z=-8-6t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413229} {:answer/id #uuid "9024db90-ca7f-5e93-98cf-00227482d43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=7 \\\\ y=2+3t \\\\ z=-8-6t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413230} {:answer/id #uuid "9177928a-2741-51e3-86bf-5cb26316adc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413232} {:answer/id #uuid "b5885116-09bb-5d3d-8d90-739f213a2b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=5t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=-8+6t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "898d0828-6322-5cc9-9782-a0f2a96d4581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=7t \\\\ y=3+2t \\\\ z=-8-6t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413229} :answer-field/id #uuid "acbe6b35-1062-5ba5-b134-eaeb97e4203f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca426087-2ced-544a-8fc8-80a74f2884ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-127074" :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{2}=\\frac{z+8}{-6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{2}=\\frac{z-(-8)}{-6},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,3,-8)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨7,2,-6⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=7t \\\\ y=3+2t \\\\ z=-8-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aa0077e-181b-5c87-b397-bf90c7b66f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=10-6t \\\\ y=-5+3t \\\\ z=-7+2t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413211} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff9e3e1-5e91-5965-a734-3ac520d19c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413212} {:answer/id #uuid "3a1b04c5-6fb6-5d48-9648-cb591bb11169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=10-6t \\\\ y=-3+5t \\\\ z=-7+2t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413214} {:answer/id #uuid "55d1767d-6142-57fd-84ac-0823297abb7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=10+6t \\\\ y=5+3t \\\\ z=7+2t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413220} {:answer/id #uuid "cbce69e5-b398-5143-8fee-8b1fc3bf565b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=10+6t \\\\ y=-5+3t \\\\ z=7-2t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aa0077e-181b-5c87-b397-bf90c7b66f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=10-6t \\\\ y=-5+3t \\\\ z=-7+2t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413211} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cb77e23-9c21-5d3c-a6f6-d6c088c776f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc81db7e-a314-5809-b5c5-608d6bc1d824" :question/math-academy-id "q-127076" :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x-10}{-6}=\\frac{y+5}{3}=\\frac{z+7}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{-6}=\\frac{y-(-5)}{3}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{2},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(10,-5,-7)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-6,3,2⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=10-6t \\\\ y=-5+3t \\\\ z=-7+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22045b16-d166-53e9-a860-057d8df291c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z-7}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260169} {:answer/id #uuid "8a88b0e9-8149-5353-aec9-2d87d5dc3d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{-1}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261083} {:answer/id #uuid "c457b05b-947b-53b9-8b81-9439d4269d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261609} {:answer/id #uuid "d13e62fc-794e-5c6c-85fc-8a02848df06b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261721} {:answer/id #uuid "df19c412-9a35-5b2b-8bcf-df577752cae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z-1}{-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c457b05b-947b-53b9-8b81-9439d4269d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261609} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f9de2d2-96e1-5828-8e77-3d0b2dfc03ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d2a6473-caac-59fb-8a5c-a6c5ea0abc7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127401" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t \\\\ y=5-t \\\\ z=-7+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-2t \\\\ 5-t \\\\ -7+t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,5,-7)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-2,-1,1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3012f4-0f16-5e89-a57e-3fda5fefd0d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260007} {:answer/id #uuid "59250e7e-0cc1-59aa-badb-e267dbd5b96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{z-2}{1},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260636} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff911b7-cf81-5f4e-bd66-a8f69e551b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260705} {:answer/id #uuid "69aca3d6-1ef4-5e05-b053-4667d30d8d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{z-2}{2},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260799} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6132d8-eb35-5131-823e-2cee5de2316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{2}=\\frac{z}{7},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ff911b7-cf81-5f4e-bd66-a8f69e551b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260705} :answer-field/id #uuid "393f9b0c-bf5c-5bb2-93a1-37868fcb5583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ca9c758-73dc-58e9-9495-123d55df1262" :question/math-academy-id "q-127454" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{7}=\\frac{y-5}{0}=\\frac{z}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{y-5}{0}=\\frac{z}{2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y-5}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=5:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3+7t \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y-5}{0}$ part and instead write $y=5$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91610606-a3e0-593c-bcc2-39d39446b5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+2}{-5}=\\frac{z}{1},\\,x=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413231} {:answer/id #uuid "a040e97c-8a79-5509-87c0-8255b799a192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+2}{1}=\\frac{z}{-5},\\,x=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413233} {:answer/id #uuid "af79ec3c-58c8-52c7-bd5a-209a42167d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-2}{1}=\\frac{z}{5},\\,x=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413237} {:answer/id #uuid "b014a37f-26e2-5e8b-b252-a864ef58a225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z}{5},\\,x=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413238} {:answer/id #uuid "c3db7411-e481-5335-9ae6-c020ef03327f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-2}{1}=\\frac{z}{-5},\\,x=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a040e97c-8a79-5509-87c0-8255b799a192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+2}{1}=\\frac{z}{-5},\\,x=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413233} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5ed977-dd6d-5306-bb26-25955c268564" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbddc051-609a-50f8-b7fd-7c6e4c6d692b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127459" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{0}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{-5}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{0}=\\frac{y+2}{1}=\\frac{z}{-5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x-7}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=7:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2+t \\\\ -5t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x-7}{0}$ part and instead write $x=7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y+2}{1}=\\frac{z}{-5},\\,x=7\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a0481f-9850-51a2-93a8-9cb1375be70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{-5}=\\frac{y+6}{2},\\,z=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413205} {:answer/id #uuid "13aa6270-4f47-57d9-96d5-e04d9559c1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{2}=\\frac{y+6}{5},\\,z=-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413208} {:answer/id #uuid "5147f586-12cc-5846-9c2a-91a6e6aae45a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{-1}=\\frac{y+6}{5},\\,z=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413219} {:answer/id #uuid "b5633467-6d30-59cb-a103-1df0f479fc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+6}{5},\\,z=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413240} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf8ec86-3cc9-58ca-b77d-738394e52afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+5}{6},\\,z=-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5633467-6d30-59cb-a103-1df0f479fc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+6}{5},\\,z=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413240} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dace27d-7465-57cd-b6cc-1a69b60f0f81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1761a52-f68d-5dfd-83a7-02fd5760a09e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127460" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-(-6)}{5}=\\frac{z-13}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+6}{5}=\\frac{z-13}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z-13}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=13:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1-2t \\\\ -6+5t \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-13}{0}$ part and instead write $z=13$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+6}{5},\\,z=13\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f85a2de-8936-53e9-b4c3-0d80c357fcab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+7}{-3}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z}{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413224} {:answer/id #uuid "83bd26ba-9b94-5213-87dd-4f816b559e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+7}{-3}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413228} {:answer/id #uuid "b23463d1-7983-5832-a155-38676beb3e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z}{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413239} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b20a42-f8fc-5421-82ef-f6666475bd47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{3}=\\frac{y+1}{4}=\\frac{z}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413248} {:answer/id #uuid "e7220639-4d6c-573b-b6b7-5fc1ded2cead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{3}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b23463d1-7983-5832-a155-38676beb3e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z}{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413239} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c9478fb-99b4-566d-b0ee-d81368042926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "999b5494-410f-5564-a5e2-d9dbe0ae34c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-127464" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through $P(7,-1,0)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-3,1,-4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{-4}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z}{-4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fafacb4-7245-53f8-8b71-32255ae24867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260306} {:answer/id #uuid "74476a0a-0598-5512-a162-969f54033e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y+4}{-5}=\\frac{z+5}{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260892} {:answer/id #uuid "8042af39-00a6-5489-835e-601f12f14e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{5}=\\frac{z+5}{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260988} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9f4224-4caa-5d08-9e45-15356341a13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{-8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261251} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaf8b10-1c90-5d3a-99e6-66bd850a2c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{-8}=\\frac{y-4}{-3}=\\frac{z-5}{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fafacb4-7245-53f8-8b71-32255ae24867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260306} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b091ac-faa9-5b51-87d6-4183663a28fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "137b0f7e-9478-507b-9673-19fabe3f3d94" :question/math-academy-id "q-127466" :question/problem "![](images/16/1647eb5cca32c6fdc99e23507db151a8299b29bfa70ebfbaa941e17fe4638586.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,4,5)$ and $Q(7,-1,2).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨7,-1,2⟩-⟨-1,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-5,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae03aa1-408b-5548-a624-6152adae5980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{-3}=\\frac{z}{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300085} {:answer/id #uuid "4906a853-7d88-56e7-b8bd-53d276e6826a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{-1}=\\frac{y-3}{1}=\\frac{z-2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300333} {:answer/id #uuid "8d335767-409a-536d-b540-907f8e291fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300676} {:answer/id #uuid "bffb27fb-b54b-539d-93bb-e073e9539a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{-1}=\\frac{y+3}{1}=\\frac{z+2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300924} {:answer/id #uuid "d51a31cb-125b-53e4-97cb-d4cb19db3894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{4}=\\frac{y+1}{-3}=\\frac{z+4}{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d335767-409a-536d-b540-907f8e291fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300676} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ed0515-d165-570b-8ae3-31ffa65820ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a883673-7d08-54f5-83e2-ff286d2a402e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32829" :question/problem "![](images/58/58076d96d149ff4521103fd15c7187bc1a58595ab20f28be92be0f79385aee61.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,1,4)$ and $Q(3,-2,2).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-2,2⟩-⟨-1,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006d023d-d46a-561d-b3fd-bba07a9fea2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=-1+3t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302449} {:answer/id #uuid "2868ee6f-e3e5-5136-8ac4-b3319b7ec9ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=4+t \\\\ z=3-t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302717} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee56b4d-bb11-5431-b54e-ebd7f7c4a517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2t \\\\ y=4t \\\\ z=3t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302748} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe923a7-f409-5385-a569-ff1543d27afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302958} {:answer/id #uuid "8cdd29cd-d11d-542d-ab31-66895e33a672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "006d023d-d46a-561d-b3fd-bba07a9fea2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=-1+3t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302449} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac59fe1-14f4-5a54-aff3-69e582242c22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf2db5ae-81e4-5762-b815-c025437def46" :question/math-academy-id "q-32936" :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{4}=\\frac{z+1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{4}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{3},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(2,1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,4,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=-1+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59f75482-4344-5455-9326-2b02a2664200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+1}{-5}=\\frac{z-6}{5},\\,x=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413221} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5f12f8-0cc6-5f24-912e-0e0046f905d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413222} {:answer/id #uuid "bab03ceb-16b2-5883-b741-7e3fa8ad18e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-1}{-5}=\\frac{z+6}{5},\\,x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413242} {:answer/id #uuid "e90875fe-b897-5ee2-a9d0-62239b411734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{6}=\\frac{y}{1}=\\frac{z}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413250} {:answer/id #uuid "f81bd765-d473-5593-847e-f0fd78d42b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{6}=\\frac{y+5}{1}=\\frac{z-5}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f81bd765-d473-5593-847e-f0fd78d42b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{6}=\\frac{y+5}{1}=\\frac{z-5}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413253} :answer-field/id #uuid "13a206c5-c050-5343-8992-9fa4de1720b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f5992b1-761e-5564-8420-76d555a44826" :question/math-academy-id "q-33005" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(0,-5,5)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨6,1,-6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-0}{6}=\\frac{y-(-5)}{1}=\\frac{z-5}{-6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{6}=\\frac{y+5}{1}=\\frac{z-5}{-6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0458b289-ce6f-53e9-9190-50822f0b8ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+7}{2}=\\frac{y+4}{3},\\,z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302482} {:answer/id #uuid "469011d3-9dbd-516d-9a6b-23bf10fe8d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302904} {:answer/id #uuid "5df0f6c3-4d3f-5166-a228-df9184328d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+7}{2}=\\frac{y+4}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303029} {:answer/id #uuid "7c80630d-4868-5757-9cc0-ceebffaf7e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303228} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f04deb-868d-57ec-99de-d67f676cd65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{7}=\\frac{y}{-4}=\\frac{z}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "469011d3-9dbd-516d-9a6b-23bf10fe8d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302904} :answer-field/id #uuid "afdffc35-a050-5be2-9abe-237a21d184e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "deb4cbaf-5918-5a60-ba36-d2bbb72dfbda" :question/math-academy-id "q-33234" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f9599192c1979f40fc519458019ed3296d9a5ef9048aaed41aab973c62e0be8.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-2,3,1)$ and $Q(5,-1,4).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,-1,4⟩-⟨-2,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-2)}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2d99a9-ddfb-5a97-abd4-e05bcb1d544a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{-4}=\\frac{y-5}{2}=\\frac{z+3}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413209} {:answer/id #uuid "6e085e60-92ea-59c0-9469-2c2512ee9670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{-4}=\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413223} {:answer/id #uuid "781dc19c-aaeb-55b8-b85e-398e8c40ddf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{3}=\\frac{y+2}{5}=\\frac{z+6}{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413225} {:answer/id #uuid "c2000bfc-3f8b-500c-ac25-9de30657d477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{-4}=\\frac{y+5}{2}=\\frac{z+3}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413243} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ee8d2d-95e8-52c8-884c-18433cf351d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{5}=\\frac{z-6}{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c2d99a9-ddfb-5a97-abd4-e05bcb1d544a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{-4}=\\frac{y-5}{2}=\\frac{z+3}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413209} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b270efb-9c32-5f2a-a9f0-0cab10121392" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf27c214-ec1e-55e8-948b-06e561f6f7e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-33246" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-4t \\\\ y=5+2t \\\\ z=-3+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3-4t \\\\ 5+2t \\\\ -3+6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4t \\\\ 2t \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(3,5,-3)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-4,2,6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-4}=\\frac{y-5}{2}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-4}=\\frac{y-5}{2}=\\frac{z+3}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009411d3-d832-5609-913e-acebd7d7bf4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{5}=\\frac{z}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336035} {:answer/id #uuid "092a7315-5896-5792-a85d-57b6c0dd114b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{2}=\\frac{y+5}{-3}=\\frac{z-6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336111} {:answer/id #uuid "462f0843-eb05-5ebd-9a15-d012956547f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{2}=\\frac{y-5}{-3}=\\frac{z+6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336684} {:answer/id #uuid "68221d0f-377e-5a6b-b460-b93c5443528c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336961} {:answer/id #uuid "bca2c6fa-f347-5c3e-83d6-457baf506743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{5}=\\frac{z+7}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68221d0f-377e-5a6b-b460-b93c5443528c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336961} :answer-field/id #uuid "457d9519-cf87-50e2-920f-0c4455b746d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cadba95b-e6bb-5c0e-9d1a-07af961b4052" :question/math-academy-id "q-71020" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+4t \\\\ y=-3+5t \\\\ z=7-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+4t \\\\ -3+5t \\\\ 7-6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 5t \\\\ -6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(2,-3,7)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨4,5,-6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4687a476-743c-5663-8059-be516a1b00d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-10}{3}=\\frac{y}{-7}=\\frac{z+6}{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336687} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee0f395-cc46-5d7d-8c23-3711b1bbc7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{y}{-7}=\\frac{z}{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336761} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb4e60c-fc92-5e6a-873c-9adb63a08f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{10}=\\frac{z+5}{-6},\\,y=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336892} {:answer/id #uuid "ae824b75-7b04-55e9-9b91-a0db68311c7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+10}{3}=\\frac{y}{-7}=\\frac{z-6}{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337598} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb80ffc-9a94-5fa5-b322-54fc8ccc30cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{10}=\\frac{z-5}{-6},\\,y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4687a476-743c-5663-8059-be516a1b00d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-10}{3}=\\frac{y}{-7}=\\frac{z+6}{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336687} :answer-field/id #uuid "e26532ed-39ca-568b-b8c1-04064e77541d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a15416a-0d95-55a8-b911-87d641acee2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71028" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(10,0,-6)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨3,-7,-5⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{3}=\\frac{y-0}{-7}=\\frac{z-(-6)}{-5}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{3}=\\frac{y}{-7}=\\frac{z+6}{-5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68dc5f00-83c7-53e1-a431-3f9ecb95097f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+9}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{-5}=\\frac{z-7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336965} {:answer/id #uuid "99e37d85-5446-5b2d-8f13-39450e565e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{9}=\\frac{y+5}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337431} {:answer/id #uuid "a40025cc-d4e3-56de-865a-c669abbecae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{9}=\\frac{y-5}{3}=\\frac{z+3}{-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337521} {:answer/id #uuid "da18075b-505b-551d-a5e1-c55e43718c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z+7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337932} {:answer/id #uuid "deeb58c9-e2b4-5a3a-942e-1cd0a54981a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da18075b-505b-551d-a5e1-c55e43718c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z+7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337932} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0db96f4-8315-5937-8bf9-ee8849260120" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "489a2b97-718c-5e30-886b-642db6f4e1ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-71030" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through $P(9,3,-7)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨4,-5,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z+7}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0b4186-9928-58b4-8819-35423e1e0068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336310} {:answer/id #uuid "50820664-a7f6-5b55-9476-029673b80c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{-3}=\\frac{y-10}{6}=\\frac{z-3}{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336771} {:answer/id #uuid "933a9135-02ba-5925-965d-ddb23e164c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+6}{10}=\\frac{z-2}{3},\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337373} {:answer/id #uuid "de1fd4dc-f550-5479-9beb-f535161eede1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337963} {:answer/id #uuid "fa860762-e327-5c0a-92c1-afb3900761b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{-3}=\\frac{y+10}{6}=\\frac{z+3}{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e0b4186-9928-58b4-8819-35423e1e0068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336310} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a12a0a6-eca3-5324-87ae-e350ea1c6ee0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d55a1be6-a075-557b-b7ae-a0512fb81218" :question/math-academy-id "q-71035" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z-(-2)}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-3:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6+10t \\\\ -2+3t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac0ca21-0772-5ea6-97f1-1ae436ee1c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=z-4,\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413207} {:answer/id #uuid "44b3c7a8-f3fd-53ad-a5d7-8900d29452ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3=z-4,\\,x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413215} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bbe4bc-2ddb-59a9-9664-3eeab65c327b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=y+3,\\,z=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413234} {:answer/id #uuid "f7eac140-f9de-54fe-86ec-955c3fb09f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=z+4,\\,y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413252} {:answer/id #uuid "fa24ccde-ab5e-541d-abe5-1fc41302c9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=y+3=z-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ac0ca21-0772-5ea6-97f1-1ae436ee1c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=z-4,\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413207} :answer-field/id #uuid "02ce321d-f42c-5876-8f33-0ad24f21e148" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f4d4cbd-f2d7-5e35-a92b-2f2fb49fe824" :question/math-academy-id "q-71037" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-0}{4}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{0}=\\frac{z-4}{1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{0}=z-4,\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y+3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=-3:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4+t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y+3}{0}$ part and instead write $y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=z-4,\\,y=-3\n$$"}]}
